{
  "chapter": "multi-agent/the-coordination-tax",
  "tier": "drill",
  "notes": [
    "Every agent carries its own prefix and re-sends its own growing transcript, so two",
    "agents are two quadratics rather than one quadratic split in half.",
    "An inbound summary joins the receiver's prefix, so it is re-read on every turn of that",
    "agent's loop. An outbound summary is written once.",
    "The multiplier is against a stated single-agent baseline, in basis points. Comparing a",
    "multi-agent system to a single agent at a different budget is comparing more compute",
    "to less compute.",
    "Latency is the one currency with a refund: parallel agents finish in the time of the",
    "slowest. The token bill is identical either way. This drill flattens the",
    "serial-parallel-serial shape of a real orchestrator into one flag.",
    "The tax is worth paying only when the task value covers the cost AND the work is",
    "genuinely parallel or genuinely must be isolated. A single agent has no tax to justify."
  ],
  "config": {
    "inputMicrosPerToken": 3,
    "outputMicrosPerToken": 15,
    "turnMs": 4000,
    "rampUpMs": 2000
  },
  "baseline": { "prefixTokens": 10000, "turns": 8, "outputPerTurn": 200 },
